  "final_text": "All four steps are done, and I read the dashboard and the rule back to confirm them.\n\n1. **Data view:** \"Kibana Sample Data Flights\" (index `kibana_sample_data_flights`) has the time field `timestamp`. Its other fields include:\n   - **Prices and delays:** `AvgTicketPrice`, `Cancelled`, `FlightDelay`, `FlightDelayMin`, `FlightDelayType`\n   - **Flight details:** `Carrier`, `FlightNum`, `DistanceKilometers`/`DistanceMiles`, `FlightTimeMin`/`FlightTimeHour`\n   - **Origin and destination:** airport ID, city, country, region, weather and location for each\n   - **Time:** `dayOfWeek`, `hour_of_day`\n2. **Dashboard:** \"S4-49540d82 Flights\" (id `0b0f6e02-a029-401c-a84e-02412f515426`) has exactly two bar-chart panels on that data view:\n   - flight count over `timestamp`\n   - flight count by `Carrier`, showing the top 10 carriers\n3. **Alert rule:** \"S4-49540d82 Cancelled flights\" (id `52ce5f2c-e3c0-4ba3-af32-7689b0441224`) is an Elasticsearch query rule. It filters on `Cancelled: true`, runs every minute, and fires when the count is more than 10 within 5 minutes. It was created enabled, so I didn't need to enable it, and it has no notification actions attached.\n4. **Edit and check:** The carrier panel is now titled \"Flights by carrier\"; the dashboard still has two panels. Reading the rule back shows it enabled, with the 1-minute schedule and \"more than 10 in 5 minutes\" settings unchanged.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
